From 3d4a155f32638364987f90b73c061cd65059f037 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 16:43:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] smf: handle the NAS join of a UE into a multicast MBS session

A PDU Session Modification Request carrying a Requested MBS container is an
MBS join (TS 23.247 V18.8.0 clause 7.2.1.3 step 1). The SMF never looked at
the IE, and the request then fell into the QoS-flow handling, which needs
exactly one flow to modify and answered a Modification Reject.

Handle it instead, in src/smf/mbs-join.c:

- parse the Requested MBS container (TS 24.501 V18.14.0 clause 9.11.4.30)
  and build the Received MBS container of the answer (clause 9.11.4.31);
- find the multicast MBS session by TMGI and add the UE's PDU session to its
  membership (clause 7.2.1.3 steps 2 and 4). The SMF and the MB-SMF are one
  process in this deployment, so the NRF discovery and the
  Nmbsmf_MBSSession_ContextStatusSubscribe of those steps are a lookup in
  this process's own context;
- answer the UpdateSMContext with the N1 container (PDU Session Modification
  Command, Received MBS container) and the N2 SM information (step 5): a PDU
  Session Resource Modify Request Transfer whose
  MBSSessionSetuporModifyRequestList (TS 38.413 V17.10.0, ProtocolIE-ID 319)
  names the MBS session the UE joined;
- ask the AMF to activate the session in the RAN when the first member joins
  (Namf_MBSCommunication N2MessageTransfer, TS 29.518 V17.8.0 clause 5.7.2.2,
  ngapIeType MBS_SES_ACT_REQ). The AMF already knew how to build the
  MULTICAST SESSION ACTIVATION REQUEST from it; nothing called it.

A join needs no PFCP exchange: 5GC shared delivery is used, and clause
7.2.1.3 NOTE 8 says no interaction with the UPF is needed for the session.

Membership lives on smf_mbs_sess_t as a list of associated PDU sessions, held
by pool id, and a released PDU session leaves every session it joined.

Unit tests in tests/unit/smf-mbs-test.c cover both container codecs in both
directions, the agreement of the NAS and NGAP TMGI encodings, and the two new
NGAP transfers, decoded back to check their IE ids and shapes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01PR4cGwmGjgMjM39K5jaxnP
